  "notes": "Bass-baritone country — \"the lowest lead voice on radio, arranged so the band vacates the low-mids.\" Every register decision here comes from that one documented problem. The keys are chosen low so that \"the chorus sits in a bass-baritone's upper range, which pushes the catalogue toward D/E/F/G with the melody rarely above D4\" — D4 is MIDI 62, and that is the melody ceiling, not an inference. The bass is \"deliberately thin and high-passed relative to the genre norm so it does not mask the vocal fundamental; root motion only, long notes,\" so the bass register floor is raised a fifth above the genre's while the ceiling stays under the chord voicing. The kit is arranged around the same problem: \"the kick is kept short and dry and the snare sits high and forward,\" with \"claps and a programmed 16th shaker\" on the party cuts and cross-stick on the ballads. The countermelody carries most of the melodic content, and that is stated: \"mid-register guitars, steel and fiddle carrying most melodic content because the vocal occupies the bottom.\" Melody is \"extremely narrow range (often a sixth), long note values, near-spoken verses; the hook is rhythmic rather than intervallic.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"tempo and register logic documented; harmonic detail generalised [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the ghost velocities, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
